Research item (read-only, no device, no tests).

Rank published sample-efficient RL approaches for our actual regime — ~40k device-bound decisions per run, 42 scalars + 9x60 upgrade rows, discrete hard-masked action set (mean ~2 legal, <=7 ever), choice-point cadence (ADR 0009), +1 per wave with no shaping, free learner compute (~3% busy) — against the incumbent `stacked-dqn`.

Deliverables:
- ranked table: approach, evidence, fit to our constraints, implementation cost behind the `Backbone` protocol, risk;
- which published assumptions break here (fixed dt, pixel inputs, simulator resets, native action masking);
- honest day-estimates for (a) BBF-style upgrade of the existing DQN, (b) DreamerV3 via a named PyTorch implementation, (c) EfficientZero-V2/MuZero;
- evidence at ~40k steps on structured/vector observations rather than Atari pixels, with gaps stated as gaps;
- ranked falsifiable plan for runs 3+ in `M2-P002`'s structure, naming the single strongest different-paradigm candidate;
- proposed replacement text for the ranking section of `docs/rl-candidates.md`.

Updates the durable artefact `docs/rl-candidates.md` (written in the M1 budget regime of 10^5-10^6 decisions at a 1 s cadence; the cadence work has since landed and the regime is now ~40k choice points).

Developer decision, 2026-09-24. The project's end goal is a portfolio benchmark:
- four learners (stacked-dqn, BBF, DreamerV3, EfficientZero V2) compared under one protocol on the real game;
- performance that clearly shows learning, measured against a human reference on the same fixed account;
- a README comparison table with per-model waves, samples and wall time to reach wave thresholds, and learning curves;
- detailed documents on each model and on the engineering journey (screen taps → instrumented bridge → measured speed-ups).

Done-condition: docs/task.md carries this as scope, with measurable outcomes, and §14 is reconciled.

