Scout findings (2026-09-27):
- The game calls the Workshop "Enhancement". The reflected Main fields include enhancementLevel, enhancementDefenseLevel and enhancementUtilityLevel (Int32[]), with name and max-level arrays per family. These come from a prior dump; the version is unconfirmed.
- No per-row in-run cost is stored in episode records today.
- ADR 0001/0005 and task.md:239 conflict with this scope, so ADR 0012 will define baseline v2.

Dispatched implementer-workshop-runway (worktree, software only, Tier C):
- rows addressed by name via enhancementName, plus a read-only row-list command;
- the write happens before begin_episode, with a held-check after round start;
- effect scalars are reported before and after the write, as independent evidence that the write took effect;
- `--workshop-level N` recorded in run and arm identity and in episode records;
- ADR 0012 and reconciliation of task.md, solution.md and environment-contract.md;
- both .so variants rebuilt into new build dirs; state/bridge/current is untouched.

Tier C review of a83c20f: ready with concerns; the native bounds and types are sound. Fixes directed with the retarget:
- refuse N>0 unless the emulator runs -read-only (the review's blocking finding: the game may autosave Workshop levels);
- tear down an instance before an N=0 run once it has taken a write;
- resolve all families before writing, and add workshop_write_unverified;
- an explicit effect gate before any v2 measurement;
- null the v1 floors at N>0;
- nits.

The tier C review verdict is **land**. There are no must-fixes.

The reviewer independently verified:
- flag-off `learn()` is bit-identical end-to-end, on CPU and CUDA, against the base source;
- the time-mode math matches a naive reference over 300 cases, with a maximum relative error of 1e-6;
- DreamerV3 is bit-identical;
- the data flow has no off-by-one.

Should-fix: the D4 end-of-span counter leans towards 1 on the multi-advance spans it was meant to check, because events are de-duplicated. Lead decision: **remove the counter**, and document the bounded bias in §9.4d instead: at most γ_s^span, about 5% on the longest early WAIT spans, and 0 on purchase spans. The implementer is applying this; landing follows.

